"# Chapter 5 — The Frame

You have been talking to Motoi through the terminal. Now you talk to him through the screen. Every picture Motoi draws lives in a frame — a rectangular grid of pixels the runtime holds for you. This chapter takes you from `no picture` to `picture with text` in four steps.

The tools are small. `begin-frame` and `end-frame` bracket a drawing pass. `text/draw` puts strings on the surface. `text/measure` tells you how wide a string will be before you draw it. `viewport` tells you how big the surface is. That is enough for a title screen. Chapter 6 will start moving pictures in a loop; here we get one picture right.

## Why the frame lib exists

A picture is easy to describe: it is a rectangle full of colored pixels. The hard part is making the description match what the screen shows. Different displays have different pixel densities, different color models, different refresh rates.

Motoi hides all of that behind a single abstraction: the surface. You paint onto the surface with whatever verbs you like. When you call `end-frame`, Motoi hands the surface to whatever backend is running — a browser canvas, a terminal grid, a headless test buffer — and asks the backend to show it. Your painting code does not know or care which backend is on the other side. This is the same shape Chapter 1 of Book of Composition calls the round-trip: the picture and the code that made it live in the same file, in the same shape, addressable from both sides.

The base surface verbs you get for free are the text ones — `text/draw` writes a string at an `(x y)` in a color and a font. Everything in this chapter is built from that one primitive plus the frame brackets. Later books add rasterized shapes; here we compose pictures out of words and positions, which is all a title screen needs.

## Problem 1 — Draw one word in the middle of the screen

Four lines. `begin-frame` opens the pass. `text/draw` writes the word. `end-frame` commits.

```
(begin-frame 'main)

(text/draw \"HELLO\" 130 120 'pink 'big)

(end-frame 'main)
;; => 0   (the word HELLO now lives on surface 'main)
```

`text/draw` takes five arguments in order: the string, an `x`, a `y`, a color, and a font. There are no keyword labels — the position is just `x` then `y`. `(130 120)` is roughly the middle of a 320×240 surface. `color:` accepts named palette colors — `'pink`, `'green`, `'brown`, `'blue`, `'yellow`, `'peach`, `'white` — and the font can be `'big`, `'default`, or `'tiny`. `end-frame` returns `0`, the number of pending paints it flushed.

That is your first picture. If you close the terminal and reopen it, the file remembers everything you wrote — the runtime does not. Painting is a fresh act every frame.

You can confirm a surface is really there:

```
(begin-frame 'main)
(text/draw \"HELLO\" 130 120 'pink 'big)
(end-frame 'main)
(surface-exists?)
;; => #t
```

## Problem 2 — A title, a subtitle, and a hint stacked at three positions

Now stack three pieces at three y positions with three fonts.

```
(begin-frame 'main)

(text/draw \"Motoi Scheme\"        90 80  'pink   'big)
(text/draw \"a small language\"    95 110 'green  'default)
(text/draw \"press any key\"       110 200 'brown  'tiny)

(end-frame 'main)
;; => 0
```

Notice the three fonts. `'big` for the title, `'default` for the subtitle, `'tiny` for the hint at the bottom. That is a common shape for a title screen — one loud element, one calm element, one small element. If it feels too crowded, drop one. If it feels too sparse, add one.

If you want to line things up, `text/measure` returns the pixel size of a string before you draw it:

```
(text/measure \"a small language\")
;; => (96 8)   ;; width 96, height 8
```

That tells you the string is 96 pixels wide. To center it on a 320-wide surface, start it at `(- 160 (/ 96 2))` = `112`:

```
(- 160 (quotient (car (text/measure \"a small language\")) 2))
;; => 112
```

Now you never guess an x — you compute it.

## Problem 3 — A splash screen composed from named subroutines

Now compose. Pull each logical element into its own named subroutine, then call them all inside one frame.

```
(define (draw-title)
  (text/draw \"Motoi Scheme\" 90 90 'pink 'big))

(define (draw-flower)
  (text/draw \"* * *\" 140 120 'peach 'default))

(define (draw-footer)
  (text/draw \"a language you can read\" 70 200 'brown 'tiny))

(begin-frame 'main)
(draw-title)
(draw-flower)
(draw-footer)
(end-frame 'main)
;; => 0
```

One pattern landed: `draw-title`, `draw-flower`, and `draw-footer` are named subroutines. None is a big function. Each is much easier to read than three anonymous `text/draw` calls buried in the middle of the frame block. Any time you find yourself painting more than one primitive for a single logical element, name it. The frame block then reads like a table of contents for the picture.

## Problem 4 (pro) — A responsive HUD that resizes with the viewport

Real screens come in different sizes. If you hard-code coordinates for `320×240`, your HUD breaks the moment someone runs your cart at `640×480`. The fix: ask the viewport for its dimensions and paint relative to them.

```
(define (draw-hud score lives)
  (let* ((vp     (viewport))
         (w      (car vp))
         (h      (cadr vp))
         (pad    8)
         (bottom (- h pad)))

    (begin-frame 'main)

    ;; top-left: score
    (text/draw (string-append \"score: \" (number->string score))
               pad pad 'pink 'default)

    ;; top-right: lives
    (text/draw (string-append \"lives: \" (number->string lives))
               (- w 60) pad 'green 'default)

    ;; bottom-center: hint
    (text/draw \"[space] jump   [<>] move\"
               (- (quotient w 2) 70) bottom 'brown 'tiny)

    (end-frame 'main)))

(draw-hud 1200 3)
;; => 0
```

`viewport` returns a list describing the current surface size; here we read `w` and `h` off the front of it and place elements relative to them. Now `draw-hud` works whether the screen is 320 wide or 3200 wide.

`let*` is like `let` except each binding can see the ones before it. That lets us define `bottom` in terms of `h` without nesting several `let`s.

## Capstone — A pause overlay that centers a message on any surface

```
(define (center-x text)
  (let ((vp (viewport)))
    (- (quotient (car vp) 2)
       (quotient (car (text/measure text)) 2))))

(define (draw-pause-overlay message)
  (let* ((vp (viewport))
         (h  (cadr vp)))

    (begin-frame 'main)

    ;; the message, centered by measuring it
    (text/draw message
               (center-x message) (quotient h 2)
               'pink 'big)

    (text/draw \"press [p] to resume\"
               (center-x \"press [p] to resume\") (+ (quotient h 2) 18)
               'green 'tiny)

    (end-frame 'main)))

(draw-pause-overlay \"paused\")
;; => 0
```

Read the composition. `center-x` measures a string and returns the x that centers it on the current surface — one small function doing one clear job. `draw-pause-overlay` calls it twice: once for the loud message, once for the small resume hint.

The whole overlay is one function. The only argument is the message. If tomorrow you want a `game over` overlay, you call `(draw-pause-overlay \"game over\")` and it centers itself. That is the shape.

